; 11-save.kurt

; `save` is the reverse of `load` (10-load.kurt): it writes the current
; theory and syntax out to a file, in a form that reconstructs the same
; state via a plain `load`. Handy for picking up a long session later
; without re-running every step, or for handing a colleague a
; self-contained snapshot of where you got to.

bool A, B
use A implies B "modus-ponens"
use A

show B
proof
    A
    B
qed

save "/tmp/kurt-tutorial-save-demo.kurt"
; writes everything above (syntax + facts) out as flat `.kurt` source --
; the path is resolved relative to the current working directory, not this
; tutorial file's own location

; the saved file reconstructs the same facts if `load`ed from a *different*
; session -- including `B` (which was *proven*, not `use`d, here; `save`
; flattens either kind of fact the same way, and gives every fact a label
; if it didn't already have one, so nothing is silently dropped by `load`'s
; own selective export). It's not meant to be `load`ed back into *this
; same* session, though -- that would just re-declare syntax and facts
; already present here. Try it from a fresh shell instead:
;
;     kurt /tmp/kurt-tutorial-save-demo.kurt

; `save` only captures the theory and syntax, not an open proof or a
; pending `show` -- call it once everything is settled, not mid-proof.
